        "explanation": "<p dir=\"ltr\"><span>Given:</span></p><ul><li value=\"1\"><span>f(.) is a function from B to C and is onto (surjective), meaning every element in C has a preimage in B.</span></li><li value=\"2\"><span>f(g(x)) is a function from A to C, and is also onto (surjective), meaning every element in C has a preimage in A.</span></li></ul><p dir=\"ltr\"><span>For f(g(x)) to be surjective, the function g(.) does not necessarily need to be surjective or injective. It only needs to map elements from A to B in a way that f can then map them to every element of C.</span></p><ul><li value=\"1\"><span>g(.) does not have to be onto because f can still cover all of C.</span></li><li value=\"2\"><span>g(.) does not have to be injective because multiple elements from A could map to the same element in B, and f can still cover all of C.</span></li></ul><p dir=\"ltr\"><span>Therefore, g(.) is not required to be injective or onto for the composition f(g(x)) to be surjective.</span></p>",
