        "natRange": null,
        "explanation": "<p dir=\"ltr\"><b><strong>Problem Understanding:</strong></b></p><p dir=\"ltr\"><span>We have two relations:</span></p><ol><li value=\"1\"><b><strong>teams(tid, tname)</strong></b><span> — stores team id and team name.</span></li><li value=\"2\"><b><strong>players(pid, pname, tid)</strong></b><span> — stores player id, player name, and the team id for each player.</span></li></ol><p dir=\"ltr\"><span>We need a tuple relational calculus query that returns the name of the players who play for the team whose name is 'MI'. This means that for each player, we should find their team and check if the team name is 'MI'.</span></p><h3 id=\"query-breakdown\" style=\"text-align:left\"><span>Query Breakdown:</span></h3><p dir=\"ltr\"><span>To retrieve the player names for the team \"MI,\" we need:</span></p><ul><li value=\"1\"><span>To find players in the </span><b><strong>players</strong></b><span> relation where the </span><b><strong>tid</strong></b><span> of the player matches the </span><b><strong>tid</strong></b><span> of a team in the </span><b><strong>teams</strong></b><span> relation.</span></li><li value=\"2\"><span>Additionally, we need to check that the </span><b><strong>tname</strong></b><span> of the team is 'MI'.</span></li></ul><p dir=\"ltr\"><span>Now, let's analyze the options:</span></p><h3 id=\"option-1\" style=\"text-align:left\"><span>Option 1:</span></h3><p dir=\"ltr\"><b><strong>{ p.pname | p ∈ players ∧ ∃t (t ∈ teams ∧ p.tid = t.tid ∧ t.tname = 'MI') }</strong></b></p><ul><li value=\"1\"><span>This option checks that for every player </span><b><strong>p</strong></b><span> in the </span><b><strong>players</strong></b><span> relation, there exists a team </span><b><strong>t</strong></b><span> in the </span><b><strong>teams</strong></b><span> relation where the player's </span><b><strong>tid</strong></b><span> matches the team's </span><b><strong>tid</strong></b><span> and the team name is 'MI'.</span></li><li value=\"2\"><span>This query correctly finds the names of players whose team name is 'MI'.</span></li></ul><h3 id=\"option-2\" style=\"text-align:left\"><span>Option 2:</span></h3><p dir=\"ltr\"><b><strong>{ p.pname | p ∈ teams ∧ ∃t (t ∈ players ∧ p.tid = t.tid ∧ t.tname = 'MI') }</strong></b></p><ul><li value=\"1\"><span>This option incorrectly uses </span><b><strong>p ∈ teams</strong></b><span>, which means it is trying to retrieve players from the </span><b><strong>teams</strong></b><span> relation. This is incorrect because </span><b><strong>teams</strong></b><span> contains team data, not player data.</span></li></ul><h3 id=\"option-3\" style=\"text-align:left\"><span>Option 3:</span></h3><p dir=\"ltr\"><b><strong>{ p.pname | p ∈ players ∧ ∃t (t ∈ teams ∧ t.tname = 'MI') }</strong></b></p><ul><li value=\"1\"><span>This option finds all players in the </span><b><strong>players</strong></b><span> relation but only checks if there exists any team with the name 'MI', without ensuring the player's team matches the team 'MI'. This query is not specific to the players whose team is 'MI'.</span></li></ul><h3 id=\"option-4\" style=\"text-align:left\"><span>Option 4:</span></h3><p dir=\"ltr\"><b><strong>{ p.pname | p ∈ teams ∧ ∃t (t ∈ players ∧ t.tname = 'MI') }</strong></b></p><ul><li value=\"1\"><span>This option, like Option 2, incorrectly uses </span><b><strong>p ∈ teams</strong></b><span>, which is not valid because we want players, not teams, to be queried.</span></li></ul><h3 id=\"conclusion\" style=\"text-align:left\"><span>Conclusion:</span></h3><p dir=\"ltr\"><span>The correct query is </span><b><strong>Option 1</strong></b><span>, as it correctly retrieves the player names whose team name is 'MI' based on the given conditions.</span></p><p dir=\"ltr\"><b><strong>Answer: Option 1</strong></b></p>",
