        "questionType": "MCQ",
        "natRange": null,
        "explanation": "<p dir=\"ltr\"><b><strong>Step 1: Analyzing the transitions and outputs</strong></b></p><p dir=\"ltr\"><span>We need to analyze which states are equivalent and could potentially be merged.</span></p><ul><li value=\"1\"><b><strong>States A and B</strong></b><span>:</span><ul><li value=\"1\"><span>A transitions to F on X=0 and to B on X=1.</span></li><li value=\"2\"><span>B transitions to D on X=0 and to C on X=1.</span></li><li value=\"3\"><span>Since their transitions and outputs are different, they are not equivalent.</span></li></ul></li><li value=\"2\"><b><strong>States C and F</strong></b><span>:</span><ul><li value=\"1\"><span>C transitions to F on X=0 and to E on X=1.</span></li><li value=\"2\"><span>F transitions to F on X=0 and to B on X=1.</span></li><li value=\"3\"><span>These states are different in terms of transitions and outputs.</span></li></ul></li><li value=\"3\"><b><strong>States D, G, H</strong></b><span>:</span><ul><li value=\"1\"><span>D transitions to G on X=0 and to A on X=1.</span></li><li value=\"2\"><span>G transitions to D on X=0 and to H on X=1.</span></li><li value=\"3\"><span>H transitions to G on X=0 and to A on X=1.</span></li><li value=\"4\"><span>These states seem related, but they have different transitions and outputs, so they cannot be merged.</span></li></ul></li></ul><h3 id=\"step-2-identifying-equivalence\" style=\"text-align:left\"><span>Step 2: Identifying equivalence</span></h3><p dir=\"ltr\"><span>After analyzing the transitions and outputs, we see that there are </span><b><strong>5 distinct states</strong></b><span> based on their behavior. The states </span><b><strong>A, B, C, D, E, F, G, H</strong></b><span> cannot be merged further due to differences in their transitions.</span></p><h3 id=\"final-answer\" style=\"text-align:left\"><span>Final Answer:</span></h3><p dir=\"ltr\"><span>The minimum number of states required to realize the FSM is </span><b><strong>5</strong></b><span>.</span></p>",
