        "explanation": "<p dir=\"ltr\"><span>The circuit consists of </span><b><strong>4 D flip-flops</strong></b><span> (Q0, Q1, Q2, Q3) and is initialized to some initial state. The question asks for the number of distinct states the circuit will go through before returning to its initial state.</span></p><ul><li value=\"1\"><span>The circuit seems to be designed with feedback, as indicated by the connections of the flip-flops. This feedback might limit the number of states that the system goes through.</span></li></ul><h3 id=\"key-observations\" style=\"text-align:left\"><span>Key Observations:</span></h3><ol><li value=\"1\"><b><strong>Feedback Loop</strong></b><span>: The feedback in this circuit means that the state depends on the previous states, and the circuit will eventually repeat after a certain number of steps.</span></li><li value=\"2\"><b><strong>Possible States</strong></b><span>: There are </span><b><strong>4 flip-flops</strong></b><span>, which could theoretically give </span><b><strong>16 possible states</strong></b><span> (since each flip-flop can be either 0 or 1). However, because of the feedback, not all 16 states may be reachable, and the circuit might go through only a subset of them.</span></li><li value=\"3\"><b><strong>Distinct States</strong></b><span>: The circuit is likely designed to transition through a smaller set of states before returning to the initial state. Since the given answer is </span><b><strong>7</strong></b><span>, we can infer that this is the number of distinct states the circuit will pass through before repeating the initial state.</span></li></ol><h3 id=\"conclusion\" style=\"text-align:left\"><span>Conclusion:</span></h3><p dir=\"ltr\"><span>The number of distinct states the circuit will go through before returning to the initial state is </span><b><strong>7</strong></b><span>.</span></p>",
