        "questionType": "MSQ",
        "natRange": null,
        "explanation": "<p dir=\"ltr\"><span>{a</span><sup><span>m</span></sup><span> b</span><sup><span>n</span></sup><span> | m, n ≥ 0} is a regular language because it describes strings where there are any number of </span><code><span>a</span></code><span>'s followed by any number of </span><code><span>b</span></code><span>'s. This can be represented by the regular expression </span><code><span>a* b*</span></code><span>, which is regular.</span></p><p dir=\"ltr\"><span>{w | w ∈ {a, b}*, #a(w) ≡ 2 (mod 7), and #b(w) ≡ 3 (mod 9)} defines a language where the number of </span><code><span>a</span></code><span>'s is congruent to 2 mod 7 and the number of </span><code><span>b</span></code><span>'s is congruent to 3 mod 9. Regular languages can count occurrences of symbols with fixed remainders modulo some number, so this is a regular language. It can be represented using regular expressions with modular arithmetic for the counts of </span><code><span>a</span></code><span> and b.</span></p>",
        "tag": "GATE 2025 || CSE || MSQ"
