        "id": 13852,
        "question": "<p dir=\"ltr\"><span>In a B+ tree the requirement of at least half full (50%) node occupancy is relaxed for which one of the following cases?</span><br><span>&nbsp;</span></p>",
        "options": [
          "<p dir=\"ltr\"><span>All leaf node&nbsp;</span></p>",
          "<p dir=\"ltr\"><span>Only the root node</span></p>",
          "<p dir=\"ltr\"><span>All internal nodes&nbsp;</span></p>",
          "<p dir=\"ltr\"><span>Only the left most leaf node</span></p>"
        ],
        "correctAnswer": 1,
        "correctAnswers": [
          1
        ],
        "questionType": "MCQ",
        "natRange": null,
        "explanation": "<p dir=\"ltr\"><span>In a </span><a href=\"https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/introduction-of-b-tree/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><span>B+ tree</span></a><span>, the root node acts as the primary access point for data. It doesn't strictly follow the occupancy requirements like internal and leaf nodes. Hence, it to be less than half-full(50%). It helps the root node efficiently manage structural changes and ensures it can handle different data volumes and maintain a balanced tree.</span></p>",
