        "questionType": "MCQ",
        "natRange": null,
        "explanation": "<ul><li value=\"1\"><span>Threads can only be implemented in kernel space is false as threads can be implemented in user space as well using mechanisms provided by kernel threads.</span></li><li value=\"2\"><span>All the threads belonging to the process share the same descriptor along with data segment, heap etc.</span></li><li value=\"3\"><span>Each thread has its own stack within the same address space, enabling them to execute in parallel.</span></li><li value=\"4\"><span>All threads within a single process can access and modify shared data, potentially causing the critical section problem. Therefore, developers must implement synchronization mechanisms to ensure safe access to these shared resources.&nbsp;</span></li></ul><p dir=\"ltr\"><span>Hence, statement :'Threads belonging to a process are by default not protected from each other' is TRUE.</span><br><span>&nbsp;</span></p>",
