        "questionType": "MSQ",
        "natRange": null,
        "explanation": "<p dir=\"ltr\"><b><strong>(A)</strong></b><span>Since the input string </span><code><span>\"abcd\"</span></code><span> has a valid null-terminated structure, the function will always return a value.</span></p><p dir=\"ltr\"><b><strong>(B)</strong></b><span>When </span><code><span>c</span></code><span> is declared as a character array, it behaves like a pointer to the first character of the string. Passing </span><code><span>c</span></code><span> to the function behaves identically to passing </span><code><span>\"abcd\"</span></code><span>. The function computes the length of the string and returns it. Hence, it will always return a value.</span></p><p dir=\"ltr\"><b><strong>(C)</strong></b><span> is </span><b><strong>incorrect</strong></b><span>. The code will compile without any issues as it uses valid pointer arithmetic, a </span><code><span>while</span></code><span> loop to traverse the string, and correctly computes and returns the difference between two pointers.</span></p><p dir=\"ltr\"><b><strong>(D)</strong></b><span> When </span><code><span>c</span></code><span> is declared as a pointer to a string literal, it points to the memory location of the string </span><code><span>\"abcd\"</span></code><span>. The function </span><code><span>fX(c)</span></code><span> processes the string and returns the length, similar to the earlier cases. Hence, it will always return a value.</span></p><p dir=\"ltr\"><span>Hence, A,B and D are correct options.</span></p>",
