        "id": 13987,
        "question": "<p dir=\"ltr\"><span>Consider a 32-bit system with 4 KB page size and page table entries of size 4 bytes each. Assume 1 KB = 210 bytes. The OS uses a 2-level page table for memory management, with the page table containing an outer page directory and an inner page table. The OS allocates a page for the outer page directory upon process creation. The OS uses demand paging when allocating memory for the inner page table, i.e., a page of the inner page table is allocated only if it contains at least one valid page table entry.</span></p><p dir=\"ltr\"><span>An active process in this system accesses 2000 unique pages during its execution, and none of the pages are swapped out to disk. After it completes the page accesses, let X denote the minimum and Y denote the maximum number of pages across the two levels of the page table of the process.</span></p><p dir=\"ltr\"><span>The value of X + Y is&nbsp;</span></p>",
