        "explanation": "<p dir=\"ltr\"><span>Two schedules, S1S_1S1​ and S2S_2S2​, are considered </span><a href=\"https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/conflict-serializability-in-dbms/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><span>conflict-equivalent</span></a><span> if the conflicting operations in both schedules are executed in the same order. Conflicting operations are defined by the pairs: Read-Write (RW), Write-Read (WR), and Write-Write (WW).</span><br><span>The conflict pairs are as follows:</span></p><ul><li value=\"1\"><span>R2(B)→W1(B)</span></li><li value=\"2\"><span>W2(B)→W1(B)</span></li><li value=\"3\"><span>R1(C)→W2(C)</span></li><li value=\"4\"><span>R2(D)→W1(D)</span></li></ul><p dir=\"ltr\"><span>A schedule where these conflict pairs are executed in the same order will be conflict equivalent to the given schedule. </span><br><span>Among the options provided, only Option 1 preserves the exact order of these conflict pairs, making it conflict equivalent to the given schedule.</span></p>",
