        "explanation": "<p dir=\"ltr\"><span>The given preorder is - 15, 10, 12, 11, 20, 18, 16, 19.</span><br><span>As we know that, the inorder traversal of a binary search tree (BST) results in a sorted list.&nbsp;</span><br><span>So, the inorder traversal will be - 10, 11, 12, 15, 16, 18, 19, 20</span><br></p><figure class=\"image\"><img src=\"https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20241226151402523232/BST.webp\" alt=\"BST\" width=\"450\" height=\"401\" srcset=\"https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20241226151402523232/BST.webp 450w,https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20241226151402523232/BST-100.webp 100w,https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20241226151402523232/BST-200.webp 200w,https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20241226151402523232/BST-300.webp 300w\" loading=\"auto\"><figcaption>BST</figcaption></figure><p dir=\"ltr\"><br></p><p dir=\"ltr\"><span>By taking help of both the traversal we can design the required BST.</span></p><p dir=\"ltr\"><span>Option (B) is correct.   </span></p>",
