        "questionType": "MCQ",
        "natRange": null,
        "explanation": "<p dir=\"ltr\"><span>I. Minimum Spanning Tree of G is always unique - MST will always be distinct if the edges are unique </span><b><strong>so Correct</strong></b><span> II. Shortest path between any two vertices of G is always unique - Shortest path between any two vertices can be same</span><b><strong> so incorrect</strong></b><span> Therefore</span><b><strong>, option A</strong></b><span> is correct&nbsp;</span><br><br><b><strong>Alternate solution:</strong></b><span> We know that minimum spanning tree of a graph is always unique if all the weight are distinct, so statement 1 is correct. Now statement 2 , this might not be true in all cases. Consider the graph.</span></p><figure class=\"image\"><img src=\"https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20241230145914654172/file.webp\" alt=\"file\" width=\"800\" height=\"400\" srcset=\"https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20241230145914654172/file.webp 800w,https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20241230145914654172/file-100.webp 100w,https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20241230145914654172/file-200.webp 200w,https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20241230145914654172/file-300.webp 300w,https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20241230145914654172/file-660.webp 660w,https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20241230145914654172/file-768.webp 768w\" loading=\"auto\"><figcaption> </figcaption></figure><p dir=\"ltr\"><br></p><p dir=\"ltr\"><span>&nbsp;&nbsp;</span><br><span>There are two shortest paths from a to b (one is direct and other via node c) So statement 2 is false Hence option a is correct.</span></p>",
