        "explanation": "<p dir=\"ltr\"><span>To form a four-digit number with non-decreasing digits from the set {1, 2, 3}, we can look at the possible combinations:</span><br><span>1. All digits are 1s: There's only one such number, 1111.</span><br><span>2. One digit is 2 and the rest are 1s: There are four such numbers, 1112, 1122, 1222, and 2222.</span><br><span>3. One digit is 3 and the rest are 1s: There are four such numbers, 1113, 1133, 1333, and 3333.</span><br><span>4. One digit is 2, one digit is 3, and the rest are 1s: There are six such numbers, 1123, 1133, 1223, 1233, 1333, and 2233.</span><br><span>Adding these up, we have a total of 1+4+4+6=15 four-digit numbers with non-decreasing digits from the set {1, 2, 3}.</span></p>",
