        "id": 7606,
        "question": "<p dir=\"ltr\"><span>Consider the following policies for preventing deadlock in a system with mutually exclusive resources. </span></p><pre><span>I. Processes should acquire all their resources at the </span><br><span>   beginning of execution.  If any resource is not  </span><br><span>   available, all resources acquired so far are released.</span><br><span>II. The resources are numbered uniquely, and processes are </span><br><span>    allowed to request for resources only in increasing </span><br><span>    resource numbers.</span><br><span>III. The resources are numbered uniquely, and processes are</span><br><span>     allowed to request  for resources only in decreasing </span><br><span>     resource numbers.</span><br><span>IV. The resources are numbered uniquely. A process is allowed</span><br><span>    to request only for a resource with resource number larger</span><br><span>    than its currently held resources.</span></pre><p dir=\"ltr\"><span> Which of the above policies can be used for preventing deadlock?</span></p>",
