        "question": "<p dir=\"ltr\"><span>A random variable X is said to be distributed as Bernoulli(θ), denoted by X ∼ Bernoulli(θ), if </span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"text-align: center;\"><span>P(X = 1) = θ, P(X = 0) = 1 − θ </span></p><p dir=\"ltr\"><span>for 0 &lt; θ &lt; 1. Let [Tex]Y=\\sum\\limits_{i=1}^{300} X_{i},[/Tex] where X</span><sub><span>i </span></sub><span>∼ Bernoulli(θ), i = 1, 2, . . . , 300 be independent and identically distributed random variables with θ = 0.25. The value of P(60 ≤ Y ≤ 90), after approximation through Central Limit Theorem, is given by  </span></p><p dir=\"ltr\"><span>(Recall that [Tex]\\phi(x) = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi}} \\int\\limits_{-\\infty}^{x} e^{-\\frac{t^{2}}{2}}dt[/Tex] )</span></p>",
