        "explanation": "<p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"text-align: justify;\"><span>The doping distribution in P-type semiconductor (in log scale) is shown below in the figures.</span></p><figure class=\"image\"><img alt=\"Screenshot-2025-09-12-165514\" height=\"189\" loading=\"auto\" src=\"https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20250912165447525140/Screenshot-2025-09-12-165514.png\" srcset=\"https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20250912165447525140/Screenshot-2025-09-12-165514.png 533w,https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20250912165447525140/Screenshot-2025-09-12-165514-100.png 100w,https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20250912165447525140/Screenshot-2025-09-12-165514-200.png 200w,https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20250912165447525140/Screenshot-2025-09-12-165514-300.png 300w\" width=\"533\"/><figcaption> </figcaption></figure><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"text-align: justify;\"><span>Applying the current density equation </span></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><span> </span><img height=\"20\" loading=\"auto\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" width=\"73\"/></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><span>\t\t0 = </span><img height=\"37\" loading=\"auto\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" width=\"101\"/></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"text-align: justify;\"><span>\t(In a Silicon sample Total current density is equal to zero)</span></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><span> </span><img height=\"37\" loading=\"auto\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" width=\"93\"/></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><span> </span><img height=\"37\" loading=\"auto\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" width=\"93\"/></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><span> </span><img height=\"39\" loading=\"auto\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" width=\"61\"/></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><span> </span><img height=\"21\" loading=\"auto\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" width=\"42\"/></p><p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><span> </span><img height=\"40\" loading=\"auto\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" width=\"66\"/><img height=\"15\" loading=\"auto\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" width=\"19\"/><span> </span><img height=\"37\" loading=\"auto\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" width=\"112\"/></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"text-align: justify;\"><span>\t\t x</span><sub><span>1 </span></sub><span>= 1 μm, x</span><sub><span>2</span></sub><span> = 2μm</span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"text-align: justify;\"><span>\tln (10</span><sup><span>14</span></sup><span>) = 32.23</span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"text-align: justify;\"><span>\tln (10</span><sup><span>16</span></sup><span>) = 36.84</span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"text-align: justify;\"><span>\t\tE = 0.026 </span><img height=\"44\" loading=\"auto\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" width=\"93\"/><span> = 1.198kV/cm</span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"text-align: justify;\"><span>\tHence, the magnitude of electric field in the semiconductor due to non-uniform doping is 1.198 kV/cm. </span></p>",
