        "id": 168230,
        "question": "<p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"text-align: justify;\"><span>\tThe figure shows an RLC circuit with a sinusoidal current source.</span></p><figure class=\"image\"><img src=\"https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20250908161112989446/Screenshot-2025-09-08-161146.png\" alt=\"Screenshot-2025-09-08-161146\" width=\"547\" height=\"157\" srcset=\"https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20250908161112989446/Screenshot-2025-09-08-161146.png 547w,https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20250908161112989446/Screenshot-2025-09-08-161146-100.png 100w,https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20250908161112989446/Screenshot-2025-09-08-161146-200.png 200w,https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20250908161112989446/Screenshot-2025-09-08-161146-300.png 300w\" loading=\"auto\"><figcaption> </figcaption></figure><p dir=\"ltr\"><span>At resonance, the ratio </span></p><img src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" width=\"28\" height=\"44\" loading=\"auto\"><p dir=\"ltr\"><span> i.e., the ratio of the magnitudes of the inductor current phasor and the resistor current phasor, is __</span></p><p dir=\"ltr\"><span>(MCQ || 2016 || MCQ || 1 mark)</span></p>",
