        "questionType": "MCQ",
        "natRange": null,
        "explanation": "<p dir=\"ltr\"><span>(A)          </span></p><figure class=\"image\"><img alt=\"Screenshot-2025-09-18-122207\" height=\"160\" loading=\"auto\" src=\"https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20250918122238410060/Screenshot-2025-09-18-122207.png\" srcset=\"https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20250918122238410060/Screenshot-2025-09-18-122207.png 383w,https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20250918122238410060/Screenshot-2025-09-18-122207-100.png 100w,https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20250918122238410060/Screenshot-2025-09-18-122207-200.png 200w,https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20250918122238410060/Screenshot-2025-09-18-122207-300.png 300w\" width=\"383\"/><figcaption> </figcaption></figure><p dir=\"ltr\"><span>Between the wires </span>[Tex]\\text{W}_1[/Tex]<span> and </span>[Tex]\\text{W}_2[/Tex]<span>, the B- fields due to I and 2I get added up.</span></p><p dir=\"ltr\"><span> </span>[Tex]\\therefore[/Tex]<span> The B- field due to </span>[Tex]\\omega_1[/Tex]<span> at a distance ‘r’ from the </span>[Tex]\\omega_1[/Tex]<span> is </span></p><p>[Tex]\\text{B}_1 = \\frac{\\mu_0\\text{I}}{2\\pi\\text{r}} \\rightarrow (1)[/Tex]</p><p dir=\"ltr\"><span>The B- field due to </span>[Tex]\\omega_2[/Tex]<span> distance ‘3r’ from the </span>[Tex]\\omega_2[/Tex]<span> is given by</span></p><p>[Tex]\\text{B}_2 = \\frac{\\mu_0(2\\text{I})}{2\\pi(3\\text{r})} \\rightarrow (2)[/Tex]</p><p dir=\"ltr\"><span> Now total magnetic field </span>[Tex](\\text{B}) = \\text{B}_1 + \\text{B}_2[/Tex]</p><p>[Tex]= \\frac{\\mu_0\\text{I}}{2\\pi}\\left(\\frac{1}{\\text{r}} + \\frac{2}{3\\text{r}}\\right)[/Tex]</p><p>[Tex]\\text{B} = \\frac{5\\mu_0\\text{I}}{6\\pi\\text{r}}\\text{ Wb}/\\text{m}^2[/Tex]</p>",
